<section xml:id="sec-application-of-sets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Application of Sets</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.1 Sets
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Apply sets to real life situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Using sets and Venn diagrams to solve real-life classification and survey problems.
         Suggested activity: Learners solve a real survey problem, e.g. how many households in a village keep cows, goats or both, using data collected from home. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLO: Core and Essential 1.1 (e); reflection (f) in lesson 6.2. Lessons 6.1 and 6.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (households keeping cows, goats or both)
         Core-only: insight-greatest-and-least-overlap, example-tea-and-dairy, exercises-overlap-challenge,
         ex-greatest-and-least-overlap (STACK), formula checks in example-traders-at-gikomba
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-sets/06-application-of-sets/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 6.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-surveys-with-two-sets">
        <title>Surveys with Two Sets</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> how to write an unknown region as <m>x</m>, and how to check that a survey is possible.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Know (Core):</alert> the greatest and least possible overlap of two groups.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> set up and solve an equation for an unknown region, and interpret the answer.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> households, homes, mobile money and bank accounts.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners solve the households problem with a Venn diagram, then with <m>x</m>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>The day before, ask each learner to find out whether five homes near them keep cows, goats, both or neither; combine the results in part (d). Learners record honestly and keep the names of homes private.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-cows-and-goats">
            <title>Cows and Goats</title>
            <statement>
                <p>In a village survey of <m>50</m> households, <m>23</m> keep cows, <m>31</m> keep goats and <m>6</m> keep neither.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Let <m>x</m> households keep both. Write the number in each of the four regions in terms of <m>x</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Cows only <m>23 - x</m>; both <m>x</m>; goats only <m>31 - x</m>; neither <m>6</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The four regions hold all <m>50</m> households. Write an equation and solve it.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(23 - x) + x + (31 - x) + 6 = 50</m>, so <m>60 - x = 50</m> and <m>x = 10</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How many households keep cows only? Goats only?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Cows only <m>13</m>; goats only <m>21</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Combine the class's data from home (on paper or in a spreadsheet) and draw the Venn diagram.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Answers depend on the class. Check that the four regions add up to the number of homes surveyed.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out the method with one unknown region.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (a) and (b) are the method: one unknown region, every region in terms of it, one equation.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Every person is in exactly one region, so the regions add up to the total.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Check the answer: no region may be negative.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-using-x-for-an-unknown-region">
            <title>Using <m>x</m> for an Unknown Region</title>
            <p>When a survey does not give the number in a region, call it <m>x</m> and write every other region in terms of <m>x</m>. Every person is in exactly one region, so the regions add up to the total. Solve the equation, then check that no region is negative and that every set total is right. If a region comes out negative, or the regions cannot add up to the total, the survey data contain a mistake.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-solar-lamps-and-water-tanks">
            <title>Solar Lamps and Water Tanks</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Of <m>70</m> homes in a village, <m>41</m> have a solar lamp, <m>33</m> have a water tank and <m>5</m> have neither. How many have both?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>x</m> homes have both. Then <m>(41 - x) + x + (33 - x) + 5 = 70</m>, so <m>79 - x = 70</m> and <m>x = 9</m>. Nine homes have both.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-regions-in-terms-of-x">
            <title>Regions in Terms of <m>x</m></title>
            <statement>
                <p>In a class of <m>48</m>, the number who play football only is three times the number who play both football and volleyball. <m>14</m> play volleyball only and <m>6</m> play neither. How many play football?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>x</m> play both, so <m>3x</m> play football only. Then <m>3x + x + 14 + 6 = 48</m>, so <m>4x = 28</m> and <m>x = 7</m>. Football: <m>3 \times 7 + 7 = 28</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-an-impossible-survey">
            <title>An Impossible Survey</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A report says that of <m>40</m> learners, <m>26</m> take subject <m>A</m>, <m>21</m> take subject <m>B</m>, <m>5</m> take both and <m>3</m> take neither. Is this possible?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>n(A \cup B) = 26 + 21 - 5 = 42</m>, which is more than the <m>40</m> learners in the class (and <m>3</m> more take neither). The report must contain an error.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <insight xml:id="insight-greatest-and-least-overlap" component="core">
            <title>Greatest and Least Overlap</title>
            <p>When only <m>n(\xi)</m>, <m>n(A)</m> and <m>n(B)</m> are known, the overlap <m>n(A \cap B)</m> can vary.
                <ul>
                    <li><p>It is greatest when the smaller set lies inside the larger one: the greatest possible <m>n(A \cap B)</m> is the smaller of <m>n(A)</m> and <m>n(B)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>It is least when <m>n(A \cup B)</m> is as big as possible. Since <m>n(A \cap B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cup B)</m> and <m>n(A \cup B) \le n(\xi)</m>, the least possible overlap is <m>n(A) + n(B) - n(\xi)</m> when this is positive, and <m>0</m> otherwise.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </insight>

        <example xml:id="example-tea-and-dairy" component="core">
            <title>Tea and Dairy</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A cooperative in Kirinyaga has <m>130</m> farmers. <m>96</m> grow tea and <m>58</m> keep dairy cows. Find the greatest and least possible numbers who do both.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Greatest: all <m>58</m> dairy farmers also grow tea, so <m>58</m>. Least: <m>96 + 58 - 130 = 24</m>, when every farmer does at least one of the two.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example <c>{2, 5, 9}</c>. On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap <c>?123</c> (or <c>123</c>), then <c>=\&lt;</c> (or <c>#+=</c>). Type the empty set as <c>{}</c>. Type a count as a whole number.</p>

