    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 6.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-surveys-with-three-sets">
        <title>Surveys with Three Sets</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the centre as <m>x</m>; the difference between <q>exactly two</q> counts and pair totals.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> solve for the centre and complete a three-set diagram.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> clubs, foods and market traders; reflect on how sets help us count.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find an unknown centre by writing every region in terms of <m>x</m>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Stress the words <q>exactly two</q>: these counts do not include the centre.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-three-clubs">
            <title>Three Clubs</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A school asked <m>40</m> learners about three clubs. <m>18</m> are in Drama, <m>19</m> in Music and <m>21</m> in the Environment club. Exactly <m>4</m> are in Drama and Music only, <m>5</m> in Drama and Environment only, and <m>5</m> in Music and Environment only. <m>2</m> are in none of the three. Let <m>x</m> be the number in all three.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Write the number in Drama only, Music only and Environment only in terms of <m>x</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Drama only <m>18 - 4 - 5 - x = 9 - x</m>; Music only <m>19 - 4 - 5 - x = 10 - x</m>; Environment only <m>21 - 5 - 5 - x = 11 - x</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Add all eight regions.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(9 - x) + (10 - x) + (11 - x) + 4 + 5 + 5 + x + 2 = 46 - 2x</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Solve <m>46 - 2x = 40</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2x = 6</m>, so <m>x = 3</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Complete the Venn diagram.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Drama only <m>6</m>, Music only <m>7</m>, Environment only <m>8</m>; the exactly-two regions <m>4</m>, <m>5</m>, <m>5</m>; centre <m>3</m>; none <m>2</m>. Check: <m>6 + 7 + 8 + 4 + 5 + 5 + 3 + 2 = 40</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To compare the two kinds of survey data for three sets.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> every region was written in <m>x</m>; the centre appears inside all three set totals.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Always check that no region is negative and that the regions add up to the total.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-finding-the-centre">
            <title>Finding the Centre</title>
            <p>Let <m>x</m> be the number in all three sets. Surveys give the overlaps in one of two ways.
                <ul>
                    <li><p><q>Exactly two</q> counts do not include the centre. Then each <q>only</q> region is a set total minus two known numbers minus <m>x</m>, and the sum of the eight regions contains <m>-2x</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Pair totals (<q>maize and beans</q>) include the centre. Then each <q>exactly two</q> region is a pair total minus <m>x</m>, and each <q>only</q> region comes out as a number plus <m>x</m>.</p></li>
                </ul>
                Either way, add the eight regions, set the sum equal to the total and solve for <m>x</m>.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-traders-at-gikomba">
            <title>Traders at Gikomba</title>
            <statement>
                <p>At Gikomba market, <m>57</m> traders each sell at least one of clothes, shoes and bags. <m>37</m> sell clothes, <m>26</m> shoes and <m>22</m> bags; <m>13</m> sell clothes and shoes, <m>11</m> clothes and bags, and <m>9</m> shoes and bags. How many sell all three? Complete the Venn diagram.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>x</m> sell all three. The pair totals include the centre, so the <q>exactly two</q> regions are <m>13 - x</m>, <m>11 - x</m> and <m>9 - x</m>. Then
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>clothes only <m>37 - (13 - x) - (11 - x) - x = 13 + x</m>;</p></li>
                        <li><p>shoes only <m>26 - (13 - x) - (9 - x) - x = 4 + x</m>;</p></li>
                        <li><p>bags only <m>22 - (11 - x) - (9 - x) - x = 2 + x</m>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                    Nobody is outside all three, so the seven regions add up to <m>57</m>:
                    <md>(13 + x) + (4 + x) + (2 + x) + (13 - x) + (11 - x) + (9 - x) + x = 52 + x = 57</md>
                    so <m>x = 5</m>. The regions are clothes only <m>18</m>, clothes and shoes only <m>8</m>, shoes only <m>9</m>, clothes and bags only <m>6</m>, all three <m>5</m>, shoes and bags only <m>4</m>, bags only <m>7</m>, none <m>0</m>.</p>
                <p component="core">Check with the formula: <m>37 + 26 + 22 - 13 - 11 - 9 + 5 = 57</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-traders-at-gikomba">
                    <caption>What <m>57</m> traders at Gikomba sell.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A three-set Venn diagram, clothes, shoes and bags, with the number of traders in each region.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Clothes only <m>18</m>; clothes and shoes only <m>8</m>; shoes only <m>9</m>; clothes and bags only <m>6</m>; all three <m>5</m>; shoes and bags only <m>4</m>; bags only <m>7</m>; none <m>0</m>.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (-2.35,2.1) {Clothes};
\draw[thick] (0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (2.35,2.1) {Shoes};
\draw[thick] (0,-0.9) circle (1.55);
\node at (1.6,-2.4) {Bags};
% regions
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.6,1.0) {$18$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,1.35) {$8$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.6,1.0) {$9$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.05,-0.55) {$6$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,0.05) {$5$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.05,-0.55) {$4$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,-1.85) {$7$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-3.4,-2.4) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <insight xml:id="insight-why-sets">
            <title>Why Sets?</title>
            <p>A set with a clear rule describes a group without any doubt about who belongs. Union, intersection, complement and difference sort a group into parts. A Venn diagram puts each person in exactly one region, so a survey can be counted without counting anyone twice, and a negative region shows at once that the data contain a mistake.</p>
        </insight>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>How do sets help us sort, describe and count the things around us? Revisit it with <xref ref="insight-why-sets"/>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Critical thinking and problem solving (operations on sets; the centre-out method); communication and collaboration (planning and running surveys in groups).</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p>Responsibility (recording survey data honestly); respect (asking classmates' consent and keeping their answers private).</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Life skills; social awareness.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example <c>{2, 5, 9}</c>. On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap <c>?123</c> (or <c>123</c>), then <c>=\&lt;</c> (or <c>#+=</c>). Type the empty set as <c>{}</c>. Type a count as a whole number.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-three-set-survey-with-an-unknown-centre">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-three-set-survey-with-an-unknown-centre" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-sets/06-application-of-sets/Three-set-survey-with-an-unknown-centre.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-surveys-with-three-sets">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-ugali-rice-chapati">
                <statement><p>At lunch, <m>58</m> learners were asked what they ate last week: <m>30</m> ate ugali, <m>22</m> rice and <m>22</m> chapati. Exactly <m>4</m> ate ugali and rice only, <m>5</m> ugali and chapati only, and <m>3</m> rice and chapati only; <m>4</m> ate none of the three. How many ate all three? How many ate ugali only?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>All three <m>4</m>; ugali only <m>17</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Let <m>x</m> eat all three. Ugali only <m>21 - x</m>, rice only <m>15 - x</m>, chapati only <m>14 - x</m>. The sum of the eight regions is <m>(21 - x) + (15 - x) + (14 - x) + 4 + 5 + 3 + x + 4 = 66 - 2x = 58</m>, so <m>x = 4</m> and ugali only is <m>17</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-hawkers">
                <statement><p><m>55</m> hawkers each sell at least one of fruit, vegetables and eggs. <m>32</m> sell fruit, <m>25</m> vegetables and <m>25</m> eggs; <m>11</m> sell fruit and vegetables, <m>12</m> fruit and eggs, and <m>9</m> vegetables and eggs. How many sell all three?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>With <m>x</m> in the centre, the only regions are <m>9 + x</m>, <m>5 + x</m> and <m>4 + x</m> and the exactly-two regions are <m>11 - x</m>, <m>12 - x</m> and <m>9 - x</m>. The seven regions add to <m>50 + x = 55</m>, so <m>x = 5</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-hawkers-exactly-one">
                <statement><p>In <xref ref="ex-hawkers"/>, how many hawkers sell exactly one kind of item?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>14 + 10 + 9 = 33</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-centre-needs-pairs">
                <title>Challenge: Is the Centre Fixed?</title>
                <statement><p>Can the number in all three sets be found from the three set totals, the overall total and the number in none alone? Explain.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>No. Different diagrams fit the same numbers. A diagram with <m>1</m> person in all three sets and <m>1</m> in <m>C</m> only, and a diagram with <m>1</m> person in <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> only and <m>1</m> in <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> only, both give <m>n(A) = 1</m>, <m>n(B) = 1</m>, <m>n(C) = 2</m> and <m>2</m> people in at least one set, but their centres are <m>1</m> and <m>0</m>. The overlap counts are needed.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-surveys-with-three-sets">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through the activity with a card of <xref ref="fig-three-set-regions"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-ugali-rice-chapati"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and the example. Assign <xref ref="ex-ugali-rice-chapati"/> to <xref ref="ex-hawkers-exactly-one"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell><xref ref="ex-centre-needs-pairs"/>. Extension: design a three-set survey for the school, collect the data and present the Venn diagram to the class.</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>
